  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAir quality will gradually improve early this morning as\nwest/southwest winds develop and scattered showers and \nthunderstorms help disperse and wash out Canadian wildfire \nsmoke. Visibility should steadily improve, with most of the \nsmoke causing reduced visibility expected to clear between 10am \nand noon. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nA cold front, currently moving across central Wisconsin and\nnorthern Michigan, is expected to reach our region later this\nevening, generally moving through the region from 5pm and 10pm.\nAs the front approaches, a squall line is expected to develop\nnear Lake Erie and track south. Damaging straight-line winds \nwill be the primary hazard. Secondary threats include quarter \nsize hail, brief embedded tornadoes along the cold front, and \nlocally heavy rainfall. \n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has placed areas north of Interstate\n70 in an Enhanced Risk (3/5) for severe weather, where the \norientation of the wind shear relative to the front is most \nfavorable for producing widespread damaging straight-line winds.\nNumerous communities could experience downed trees and \nscattered power outages this evening as the squall line moves \nthrough. \n\nAhead of the approaching cold front, a few discrete\nthunderstorms may develop. If these storms produce updrafts that\nrapidly strengthen they could generate strong, ice-loaded \ndowndrafts capable of producing severe wind gusts. \n\nThe probability of thunderstorms is expected to decrease between\n9pm and 11pm this evening as the cold front exits south into\ncentral West Virginia. \n\nQuiet and dry conditions are expected Sunday. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nMachine learning guidance has remained consistent in signaling\nthat a low pressure system tracking across the Great Lakes on\nTuesday will bring another threat of severe storms to the\nregion. The Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has outlined the\nentire forecast domain in a 15% severe weather risk. Damaging\nwind gusts and tornadoes appear to the be the primary hazard as\nstrong wind shear accompanies this large-scale system. \n\nIn addition to the severe weather threat, there is also the\npotential for training showers and storms with a potential\nmesoscale convection system (MCS) across the Ohio River Valley.\nRainfall totals could range between 1.5-2 inches in a short \nperiod of time and result in flash flooding.",
